  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1110 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nEvening GOES Water Vapor satellite trends and 00Z upper air\nanalysis placed a weak southern stream upper-level trough over\nnortheast Texas this evening. Regional WSR-88D radar trends show\na few light returns over east Arkansas. However, confidence\nremains low that this activity is producing anything more than a\nfew sprinkles at best. Skies were mostly clear across the Mid-\nSouth with late evening temperatures in the upper 40s to middle\n50s.\n\nThis southern stream trough will continue to weaken as it moves\ninto the Lower Mississippi Valley overnight into Wednesday\nmorning. There is a low chance (20-30%) of rain showers overnight\ninto Wednesday morning, mostly over north Mississippi. Short-term\nmodels continue to show a northern stream mid-level trough and an\nassociated cold front rotating through portions of the Lower\nMississippi and Tennessee Valley late Wednesday afternoon into\nWednesday night. Short term models (CAMs, REFS, GFS, and ECMWF)\nsuggest isolated convective initiation may occur across portions\nof West Tennessee near the Tennessee River late Wednesday\nafternoon into early Wednesday evening. The Mid-South likely will\nbe on the southern fringe of any conditional convective\ndevelopment with better forcing displaced to the north and east\nof the forecast area. The 00Z REFS indicates a medium to high\nchance (greater than 50%) of joint probabilities of surface-based\nCAPE values greater than 500 J/kg, and shear in excess of 30 kts.\nHowever, this activity will be occurring closer to sunset with\nmoisture remaining limited at best. There is a low (20% or less)\nprobability of an isolated strong to severe thunderstorm should\nany convective initiation occur in this marginally favorable\nenvironment with damaging winds and large hail as the threats\nover this area.\n\nA secondary upper-level trough and associated cold front will\nrotate through the region by Thursday, bringing the coldest air\nof the season thus far. Latest NBM and LREF probabilities\nindicate a medium to high chance for temperatures to be at or\nbelow 32 degrees across much of the Mid-South by Saturday\nmorning, and a medium chance (30-50%) for temperatures to fall to\n28 degrees or lower. This cold air will likely bring an end to\nplant growth for the season but won't warrant any freeze watches\nor warnings as the official growing season ended on October 31st.\n\nA gradual warming trend is expected next week as an upper-level\nridge builds across the Middle and Lower Mississippi Valleys. A\nmodest increase in moisture with southwest flow aloft returning\nwill bring rain chances back to the region next week.",
